    "p:A MOV file and an MP4 file are almost the same thing: both are boxes of the same shape holding video and audio streams. The difference is what is inside. iPhones and Macs often put HEVC (H.265) video into a MOV, and HEVC is what Windows, Android and most web players refuse.",
    "h:What the conversion does",
    "p:Convert Files re-encodes the video to H.264 and the audio to AAC, then writes an MP4. That pair plays on essentially every device, TV, browser and messaging app. It is a real re-encode: every frame is decoded and encoded again inside your browser, so a two-minute clip takes a minute or two and the tab has to stay open.",
    "h:Settings that matter",
    "ul:Width — leave at 0 to keep the size, or set 1280 for a much smaller file that still looks good on a phone|Frames per second — 30 is right for almost everything; 60 doubles the work for sports and games footage|Quality — 85% is the sweet spot; lower if the file must be tiny",
    "h:WebM instead?",
    "p:If the video is going onto a website you build, <code>.webm</code> (VP9 + Opus) is usually smaller than MP4 at the same quality and is supported by all modern browsers. For anything else, MP4. And if a WebM has to become a picture instead — a poster frame or a thumbnail — <a href=\"/webm-to-png/\">WEBM to PNG</a> saves one frame as a PNG.",
    "p:Only the audio? Choose <code>.mp3</code> or <code>.m4a</code> as the target and the video track is dropped entirely.",
    "p:Only a picture? If all you need is one still frame from the clip — a thumbnail, a cover image, a moment to share as a photo — <a href=\"/mov-to-jpg/\">MOV to JPG</a> pulls that frame out as a JPG.",
    "h:Container and codec are not the same thing",
    "p:This is the whole confusion in one sentence: MOV and MP4 are boxes, and H.264, HEVC and AV1 are what goes in them. MP4 was standardised <em>from</em> Apple's QuickTime file format, so the two boxes are nearly the same shape internally. A .mov and a .mp4 from the same iPhone very often contain a byte-identical video stream, differing mainly in the label on the outside.",
    "p:Which is why the failure is so infuriating. The player can decode the video perfectly well. It just does not like the box.",
    "h:So why does the MOV refuse to play?",
    "p:Usually one of three reasons. The program checks the extension and declines before it even opens the file &mdash; common in upload forms and older editors. Or the container holds QuickTime-specific structures, such as timecode or closed-caption tracks, that the player does not expect and gives up on. Or, most often on recent iPhones, the video inside is HEVC rather than H.264, and the player has no HEVC decoder &mdash; the same patent-licensing story that makes HEIC photos hard to open.",
    "h:H.264 is the one that plays everywhere",
    "p:If a video has to work on an unknown device, MP4 with H.264 video and AAC audio is the combination with the widest support of anything in existence: phones, TVs, browsers, editors, projectors, ten-year-old laptops. HEVC and AV1 are both more efficient &mdash; meaningfully smaller at the same quality &mdash; but each is a gamble on the far end having a decoder. Efficiency is worth a lot on a website you control and nothing at all in an email attachment.",
    "h:What actually happens when you convert",
    "p:Every frame is decoded and re-encoded, in your browser, using your machine's processor. That is why a long clip takes real time, roughly comparable to the length of the video for high-definition footage, and why the tab needs to stay open and in the foreground while it works. It also means this is a lossy step: you are re-compressing already-compressed video, so work from the highest quality source you have rather than from something already converted twice.",
    "h:Making the file smaller while you are at it",
    "p:Resolution is the biggest lever by a wide margin. Setting the width to 1280 takes a 4K clip to roughly a sixteenth of the pixel count, and on a phone screen the result is indistinguishable. Frame rate is the second: 60fps footage of someone talking looks exactly the same at 30. Bitrate is the fiddly one &mdash; adjust the first two before reaching for it.",
